WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.020
When you think of Disney movies, you think
of safe, wholesome fun for the entire family.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.840
But based on this list, maybe instead you
should think about phone sex, sad orphans,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.099
and the literal embodiment of death appearing
in a man's eyes shortly before he meets his

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:15.200
grisly demise.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.260
That rhymed.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.320
“You know, for kids.”

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.340
Here are some of the most controversial moments
hidden in Disney movies...

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.600
All The Dead Moms

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.770
The number of dead or missing Disney moms
is staggering.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.710
Just a few of the Disney movies featuring
missing mommies: Beauty and the Beast, Pocahontas,

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:37.660
Fox and the Hound, and of course, Bambi.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.660
What gives?

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.140
Apparently Walt Disney had a devastating personal
reason why his films were so heavy-handed

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.469
with this form of despair.

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:49.100
Disney producer Don Hahn explained in an interview
with Glamour, that in the 1940s, Walt was

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.359
so successful that he bought his parents a
house.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:55.609
"He had the studio guys come over and fix
the furnace, but when his mom and dad moved

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:58.019
in, the furnace leaked and his mother died.

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:01.559
[...] He never spoke about that time because
he personally felt responsible because he

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:04.559
had become so successful that he said, "Let
me buy you a house."

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.590
It's every kid's dream to buy their parents
a house and just through a strange freak of

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:12.450
nature—through no fault of his own—the
studio workers didn't know what they were

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:12.940
doing."

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:13.520
Eesh.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.680
Still, it's not as though the dead parents
in these movies are getting eaten or anything.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:18.680
Right?

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:19.870
Family Matters

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.160
The three little pigs might have been most
afraid of the big, bad wolf coming to blow

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:28.600
their houses down, but the 1932 short film,
Three Little Pigs, also included a gentle

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.950
reminder of the other big threat to the pink
trio's safety: the human diet.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.650
Take a look at the picture labeled "Father"
hanging on the wall.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.840
Yup: that's just a length of sausage links.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.090
It doesn't stop there, either.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.850
Not long after, we see another photo of their
Piggy Papa—this time as a big ham hock.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.700
Meanwhile, in her portrait, Ma Pig was shown
nursing seven piglets rather than just those

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.700
three.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.360
Did four of the characters' siblings end up
in the slaughterhouse with dear ol' dad?

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.360
Probably!

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.260
That's all pretty gross for a kids cartoon,
right?

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.700
But at least it wasn't something truly disturbing,
like a guy dressed as Santa Claus inspiring

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.790
kids to dial a phone sex line.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.880
Uh, wait a second...

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.560
The Spanka Claus

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.170
Disney had a pretty big oopsie on its hands
with 1994's The Santa Clause.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.310
In the film, Tim Allen's Scott Calvin was
given a phone number with which he could reach

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.219
his ex-wife during his Christmas visit with
their son.

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:19.970
And so he said…

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.480
“1-800-Spank-Me, I know that number.”

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.480
As far as zingers go, it's pretty tame.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.980
But little did Disney realize the number was
actually an active sex hotline that charged

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.230
callers by the minute.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:36.540
The Seattle Times reported that several children
reportedly dialed in and racked up some huge

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.909
phone bills as a result of their curiosity.

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:43.790
The dialogue was later cut from DVD releases
for the movie and changed to "1-800-POUND"

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.560
in television airings of the same.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.989
Not that 1-800-POUND isn't any less suggestive...but
at least it's not enough numbers to actually

00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:51.439
connect to a phone sex line.

00:02:51.439 --> 00:02:54.959
So Disney accidentally got some toddlers to
call dirty chat lines.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:55.959
Big deal!

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:59.940
It's not like they inadvertently filled their
animated movies with all kinds of sexually

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.420
suggestive images and dialogue.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.420
Right?

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.359
Good Kitty, Bad Centaur

00:03:05.359 --> 00:03:09.529
Between the topless photo that found its way
into The Rescuers, and the strange anatomy

00:03:09.529 --> 00:03:14.159
that fans discovered in The Little Mermaid,
Disney movies have no shortage of accidental

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.159
sexiness.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:18.590
Case in point, 1992's Aladdin and 1997's Hercules.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.090
Legend has it that Aladdin, disguised as a
Prince, said something pretty...insane while

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.469
attempting to court Princess Jasmine on her
balcony.

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:26.469
Take a listen:

00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:28.870
“good teenagers, Take off your clothes.”

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.900
Did he say "good teenagers, take off your
clothes"?

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.900
Listen again:

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.580
“good teenagers, Take off your clothes.”

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.549
Actor Scott Weinger, who voiced the character,
has insisted he never said the words so many

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:42.750
heard...but the sound bite is still pretty
convincing despite his denial.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.849
The scripted line for the moment in question
was reportedly supposed to be "c'mon, good

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:50.400
kitty, take off and go," which would make
sense, since Aladdin's in danger of getting

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.510
eaten by a tiger.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.079
But whether due to a sound mixing flub, as
some suspect, or an intentionally implanted

00:03:56.079 --> 00:03:59.829
bit of subliminal messaging, that is just
not what what the utterance sounds like.

00:03:59.829 --> 00:04:04.700
Meanwhile, in Hercules, fans looking for more
subliminal shenanigans saw privates everywhere.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:09.530
From an alleged man-doodle on Hercules's baby
crib, to one of the Muses seemingly (but not

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:13.690
really) going commando, viewers have filled
the internet with so-called "proof" of sexual

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:17.470
deviancy sprinkled throughout the movie, but
have mostly came up with nothing.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.970
Well, except for maybe this one scene where
Hercules fights a centaur...

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.000
“Those are balls.”

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.100
Really?

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.300
Couldn't it be a mistake?

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.440
“Nope, you’re looking at balls.”

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.620
Okay, fine.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.180
We'll chalk that up to an animator getting
a little careless with his...pencil.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.880
But seriously, it's not as though Disney is
intentionally cramming adult subject matter

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.949
into its kids movies, right?

00:04:57.949 --> 00:04:59.379
Gaston Sees Death

00:04:59.379 --> 00:05:03.509
When Gaston finally goes by the wayside—as
in, getting pushed off a cliff after trying

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:07.509
to kill Belle's true love in Beauty and the
Beast, we never see his ultimate fate, even

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:10.020
though it seems pretty clear that he's met
his maker.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.520
But upon closer inspection, the scene was
even more grim than it seemed.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.970
In the close-up of Gaston's eyes, a pair of
skulls can be seen flashing in his pupils—a

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:21.860
detail deleted from VHS release but seen in
the DVD version.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.599
The fact that Disney removed it from the first
home release of Beauty and the Beast proves

00:05:25.599 --> 00:05:29.090
that someone at the studio realized that this
is actually pretty messed up.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.860
For one, the fact that it only lasts a frame
or two seems to prove that all those crazy

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.530
conspiracy theorists are right—that Disney
is sticking subliminal messages into its movies.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:39.460
For another, it's just super creepy on its
own.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.169
Do kids really need to know that Gaston is
actually seeing the face of the Grim Reaper

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:45.970
himself as he clearly plunges to his doom?

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:46.970
Probably not.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.580
Just make the candlestick dance and sing again,
okay?

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:56.759
“Be our guest, be our guest, put our service to the test.”

00:05:56.759 --> 00:05:57.849
Thanks for watching!

00:05:57.849 --> 00:06:01.090
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.889
And leave us a comment to let us know which
controversy you think was actually blown out

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:05.809
of proportion.

